[Music]  this morning we meet two refugees  bringing new flavors and opportunities  to detroit the inspiring couple from  east africa is behind one of the hottest  restaurants in the motor city nancy chen  shows us how they're also creating a  better future for others who are  starting over  along detroit's main drag hello how are  you  flavors of east africa  nadia bere and her husband mamba hamisi  bring the dishes of their native burundi  to the motor city we started to do the  pop-ups before we we opened a restaurant  and you would sell out yes in 30 minutes  people was like  what is this food how do you cook this  food i just put love in it that's it  that community support led the couple to  start their own restaurant this spring  its name baba fair what does baba mean  it's a tree growing  in west africa it grow without water  it's but it's growing  us  coming in this country as refugees we  didn't know anybody but see where we are  now  was forced to flee burundi in 2013 after  facing persecution for her human rights  work defending women it was a challenge  in many ways you don't know anybody  you discover you're pregnant  not only with one kid two kids in a  shelter it was like a new life burundi  central east hamisi was stuck in burundi  unable to get a visa raising their  daughters alone nijin bere found support  at freedom house a haven for asylum  seekers hamisi finally joined her in  detroit two years later but the former  sales and marketing manager didn't speak  english and had trouble getting hired  sadly  everything was going wrong  and then like you know what the only way  to get off this situation is to create a  business i used to say it was a lack of  opportunity and then you try to find  that exit  to  survive  finding their roadmap through family  recipes they crafted balboa fair's menu  together between the two of you who's  the better cook  who runs the kitchen she is she's in the  kitchen  she is he is good to sell my food  the only job i do is to talk  yes  creating good food isn't their only  priority the couple partners with  freedom house and other local groups to  hire refugees from around the world it's  not just a restaurant you say it's also  a gathering place it's also a safe space  that's important to you very important  coming in here with a lack of  opportunities the first thing to have in  america is to get the first job nobody  wants to hire you because you don't  speak the language you don't have the  skills and then people don't have time  for you to teach you baba fe is there to  give everybody who are coming as  refugees in this country the first  chance to know the american work  environment  people like satya duwali who was born in  somalia and is the mother of two i feel  like  with family  they understand me  and  ask me  anytime you need help or you need  something you can come to us  don't worry we're here for you  and i'm so happy  nishimbere and hamisi were granted  asylum in 2017. their journey that began  in burundi has brought them home how  does it feel to be so warmly embraced by  detroit  that's why we say this right in yambani  that is our slogan this rate is home  we can't think in detroit enough we got  our  asylum here we got our kid here  we are operating a successful business  here  we are growing here  and so yeah detroit new york as as we we  say  for cbs mornings i'm niti chen in  detroit  michigan boy sounds like when you're in  detroit you need to go to baba fair  that's right i love the back story and i  like the food i'm originally from  seattle but i spent four years in  detroit and enjoyed it  thank you nancy

